\section*{Setup \&  Dynamics}

Suppose that $\bm X:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^N$ denotes the state of a system, and let $q_i:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ be the corresponding $i^{\text{th}}$ degree of freedom. The $i^{\text{th}}$ sub-state of the system is thus given by the pairing $x_i=(q_i,\dot q_i)$. Provided that there are $n$ such degrees of freedom, which implies $N=2n$, without loss of generality, we may write:
\[
\bm X=
    [x_1,\dots,x_n]^T
     =  
    \left[
    [q_1,\dot q_1],\dots,[q_n,\dot q_n]\vphantom{\frac{.}{.}}
    \right]^T
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\bm{\dot X}=
[\dot x_1,\dots,\dot x_n]^T
=
\left[
    [\dot q_1,\ddot q_1],\dots,[\dot q_n,\ddot q_n]\vphantom{\frac{.}{.}}
    \right]^T
\]
Suppose that a controller can be implemented in order to influence the dynamics of the system. In this regard, let $\bm u:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^m$ denote the control variable from the collection of admissible controls $\mathcal{U}$ such that the $i^\text{th}$ controller input is given by $u_i:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$. As there are $m$ such inputs, we may likewise write:
\[
\bm u=[u_1,\dots,u_m]^T
\]

For any deterministic system, its dynamics can be encoded by a function of the form $f:\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^N\times\mathbb{R}^m\to\mathbb{R}^N$ (Lipschitz continuous). In particular, at the time $t$, the state of the system evolves according to the following initial value problem (IVP) in state-space form:
\[
\bm{\dot X}(t)=f(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))
\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad
\bm X(t_0)=\bm X_0
\]
This system can be visualized via the diagram below. There, at the time $t$, the (initialized) state variable $\bm X(t)$ and the control variable $\bm u(t)$ are fed into the function $f(t,x,u)$ from which the state velocity $\bm{\dot X}(t)$ is outputted. The output is then integrated, which subsequently updates the current state for feedback $\otimes$.
