\section*{Controllability}
Given any system of the form above, it is deemed to be controllable within the time interval $[t_0,t_1)$ if at any time $t\in[t_0,t_1)$ corresponding to the state $\bm{X}(t)\in\mathbb{R}^N$, and for any target state $\bm X_T\in\mathbb{R}^N$, there exists a finite duration $\delta t\in(0,t_1-t)$ with an admissible control $\bm u:[t,t+\delta t)\to\mathbb{R}^m$ such that $\bm X(t+\delta t)=\bm X_T$.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Assuming that the controller inputs are uncoupled, there exists functions of the form $g_i:\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^N\to\mathbb{R}^N$, for $i\in\{0,\dots,m\}$, such that the dynamics of the system can be decomposed into the following form:
\[
f(t,x,u)=f_0(t,x)+\sum_{i=1}^mu_ig_i(t,x)
\]

\newpage

If for every valid state pair $(t,x)\in\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^N$ one has that $\mathrm{span}\{g_i(t,x)\}_{i=1}^m=\mathbb{R}^N$,
then state space is locally accessible because the admissible controls may direct the state velocity in any state direction in $\mathbb{R}^N$. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Unfortunately, this condition is very strong, and most systems cannot admit to it. However, this condition can be significantly relaxed. Even if the state velocity does not immediately span $\mathbb{R}^N$, additional reachable directions may be synthesized via tuned successive control actuation. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
These emergent directions are characterized by the Lie bracket (i.e. the commutator), which is denoted by $[\cdot,\cdot]:C^1(\mathbb{R}^N,\mathbb{R}^N)\times C^1(\mathbb{R}^N,\mathbb{R}^N)\to C^1(\mathbb{R}^N,\mathbb{R}^N)$. In particular, the Lie bracket is defined via the following operation between any two (valid) vector fields $\bm Y$ and $\bm Z$ as below: 
\vspace{2 mm}
\[
[\bm Y,\bm Z]\coloneq\frac{\partial\bm Z}{\partial x}\bm Y-\frac{\partial\bm Y}{\partial x}\bm Z
\]\\
The Lie bracket $[\bm Y, \bm Z]$ quantifies the failure of the flows along $\bm Y$ and $\bm Z$ to commute. Specifically, it measures the first order displacement resulting from following the flow of $\bm Y$ then $\bm Z$, versus the reverse order.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
In this regard, consider the collection of vector fields that contains the dynamic functions that are coupled with the controller variable (i.e. $g_i$) in conjunction with all the novel Lie bracket combinations (under closure) between those functions and the inherent dynamical drift  of the system (i.e. $f_0$):
\[
\mathcal{G}=\bigcup_{k=1}^\infty\mathcal{G}_k
\qquad\text{with}\qquad
\mathcal{G}_{k+1}=\mathcal{G}_k\,\cup\,\left\{[f_0,\nu]:\nu\in\mathcal{G}_k\right\}\,\cup\,\left\{[\mu,\nu]:\mu\in\mathcal{G}_1,\nu\in\mathcal{G}_k\right\}
\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad
\mathcal{G}_1=\{g_i\}_{i=1}^m
\]
The collection $\mathcal{G}$ is the effective generating set for all the possible state-velocity directions that can be produced by successive controller actuation. Now, Suppose that for every valid state pair $(t,x)\in\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^N$ throughout state (pair) space, one has the following condition satisfied:
\[
\boxed{\;\mathrm{span}(\mathcal{G}(t,x))=\mathbb{R}^N\vphantom{\frac{a}{a}}\;} 
\]
Consequently, the system has the ability to reach any local state via control implementation, which yields local accessibility over state space. Furthermore, if solutions exist for all admissible controls, then reachable states are well-defined globally. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Lastly, given any two states $\bm{X}_\alpha$ and $\bm{X}_\beta$, if $\bm{X}_\beta$ being reachable from $\bm{X}_\alpha$ implies that $\bm{X}_\alpha$ is a reachable from $\bm{X}_\beta$, then the system is reachable-set symmetric. Local accessibility in conjunction with reachable-set symmetry remarkably implies controllability over state-space within the context of these notes.  

\section*{Linear Time Invariant Systems}
Given a system, it is considered to be linear time invariant (LTI) if $\partial_tf(t,x,u)=0$, which is to say the dynamics have no explicit temporal dependence, and $f(t,x,u)$ is linear in $x$ and $u$. Equivalently, this can be casted in context as such:
\[
f(t,x,u)=Ax+Bu\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad A\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}\qquad\text{and}\qquad B\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times m}
\]
From the theory of differential equations, every such system admits a unique global solution on the time interval $[t_0,t_1)$ given that the following criterion  pertaining to the controller is satisfied:
\[
\int_{t_0}^{t_1}||\bm{u}(t)||\,dt<\infty
\qquad\text{where}\qquad
||\bm u||=\sqrt{\bm u^T\bm u}
\]
Under this condition, via the formal exponential function $e^{(\cdot)}:M\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}\mapsto\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac{M^k}{k!}$, upon taking the initial condition into consideration, the general solution at every time $t\in[t_0,t_1)$ can be written as such:
\[\boxed{\;
\bm X(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\bm X_0+\int_{t_0}^t e^{A(t-\tau)}B\bm u(\tau)\,d\tau\vphantom{\frac{\frac{I}{I}}{\frac{I}{I}}}
\;}\]

\newpage

\section*{Kalman Controllability Criterion }
Here, the criterion  for an LTI system being controllable will be determined. Firstly, we observe that the dynamics of any such LTI system of the form $f(t,x,u)=Ax+Bu$ can be rewritten as follows:
\[
f(t,x,u)=Ax+\sum_{i=1}^mu_i\bm b_i
\qquad\text{where}\qquad
B=[\bm b_1\cdots\bm b_m]
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
\bm b_i\in\mathbb{R}^N
\]
This is all to say that there exists a corresponding set $f_0\cup\{g_i\}_{i=1}^m$ from the previous context for every LTI system as shown explicitly below:
\[
f_0(t,x)=Ax\qquad\text{and}\qquad g_i(t,x)=\bm b_i\qquad\text{for}\qquad i\in\{1,\dots,m\}
\]
Because $\bm b_i\in\mathbb{R}^N$, it directly follows that $[g_i,g_j]=0$. Therefore, when constructing $\mathcal{G}$, we need only consider the collection $\{\bm b_i\}_{i=1}^m$ and the Lie brackets pertaining to $f_0$ that do not vanish. As a base case, observe:
\[
[f_0,g_j]=\frac{\partial g_j}{\partial x}f_0-\frac{\partial f_0}{\partial x}g_j=\cancel{\frac{\partial\bm b_j}{\partial x}Ax}-\frac{\partial}{\partial x}(Ax)\bm b_j=-A\bm b_j\qquad\text{for}\qquad j\in\{1,\dots,m\}
\]
It follows directly from induction that any arbitrary Lie bracket composition involving $f_0$ of the following form can be determined accordingly:
\[
\underbrace{[f_0,[f_0,\dots,[f_0,g_j]]]}_\text{$k$ brackets}=(-1)^kA^k\bm b_j
\quad\implies\quad
[g_i,\underbrace{[f_0,[f_0,\dots,[f_0,g_j]]]}_\text{$k$ brackets}]=[\bm b_i,(-1)^kA^k\bm b_j]=0
\]
All other higher order bracket compositions thus vanish. Therefore, the only vectors that contribute to the set $\mathcal{G}$ are the elements of $\{\bm b_i\}_{i=1}^m$ by default, $\{(-1)^kA^k\bm b_j:j\in\{1,\dots,m\},k\in\mathbb{N}\}$ as above, and $\{0\}$ due to all the vanishing terms. This is to say:
\[
\mathcal{G}=\{\bm b_i\}_{i=1}^m\,\cup\,\{(-1)^kA^k\bm b_j:j\in\{1,\dots,m\},k\in\mathbb{N}\}\,\cup\,\{0\}
\]
We can now determine to what degree $\mathcal{G}$ generates $\mathbb{R}^N$ by taking its span, and simplifying its elements. Firstly, observe $0\in\mathcal{G}$ does not contribute to $\mathrm{span}(\mathcal{G})$ as, by definition, it is already implicit:
\[
\mathrm{span}(\mathcal{G})=\mathrm{span}\left(\left\{A^kb_j:j\in\{1,\dots,m\},k\in\mathbb{N}_0\right\}\right)
\]
Moreover, by the Cayley Hamilton Theorem, because $A\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}$, for all $k\geq N$, one has that $A^k\bm b_j$ is within the span of $\{A^k\bm b_j:j\in\{1,\dots,m\},k\in\{0,\dots,N-1\}\}$, and so we can further reduce the quantity of elements needed to construct the set generated by $\mathcal{G}$:
\[
\mathrm{span}(\mathcal{G})
=\mathrm{span}\left(\left\{A^k\bm b_j:j\in\{1,\dots,m\},k\in\{0,\dots,N-1\}\right\}\right)
=\mathrm{Col}
\begin{bmatrix}
B & AB & A^2B & \cdots & A^{N-1}B
\end{bmatrix}
\]
With all said, by definition of rank (of a matrix), the constraint $\mathrm{span}(\mathcal{G})=\mathbb{R}^N$ can be reduced to the more manageable condition, which is known as the Kalman Controllability Criterion (KCC):
\[
\mathrm{rank}
\begin{bmatrix}
\;B & AB & A^2B & \cdots & A^{N-1}B\,\;
\end{bmatrix}
=N
\]
This is to say, if the KCC is satisfied, the system is locally accessible over state space. Now, because the system is LTI, not only is it guaranteed to have a well-defined global solution (for bounded control inputs), but it is also reachable-set symmetric:
\[
\bm X_\beta = e^{A(t_\beta-t_\alpha)}\bm X_\alpha + \int_{t_\alpha}^{t_\beta} e^{A(t_\beta-\tau)}B\bm u(\tau)\,d\tau 
\quad\implies\quad
\bm X_\alpha = e^{-A(t_\beta-t_\alpha)}\bm X_\beta - \int_{t_\alpha}^{t_\beta} e^{-A(\tau-t_\alpha)}B\,\bm u(t_\beta+t_\alpha-\tau)\,d\tau
\]
It thereby follows that the KCC is both necessary and sufficient for LTI controllability over state-space:
\[
\boxed{\;\;
\mathrm{rank}
\begin{bmatrix}
\;B & AB & A^2B & \cdots & A^{N-1}B\;
\end{bmatrix}
=N
\quad\iff\quad
\text{Controllability}\vphantom{\frac{1}{1}}
\:\;}
\]

