\section*{Optimization Metric}
For every controllable system, an associated optimization problem can be posed in which the controller is to be tuned in order to minimize an objective cost. In this regard, we consider a cost functional $J$, which is defined over the time interval $[t_0,t_1)$, of the following form:
\[
J[\bm X,\bm u]=\Phi(\bm X(t_1))+\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\mathcal{L}(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))\,dt
\]
Here, $\Phi(\bm X(t_1))$ is the terminal cost and $\mathcal{L}$ is the running cost density. Now, although the state variable $\bm X$ is determined by the control variable $\bm u$ via the dynamical constraint, we nonetheless treat $\bm X$ and $\bm u$ as independent variables in order to streamline the analysis.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
In order to tune the controller $\bm{u}$, we envision a black box mechanism that receives the current state-variable data $\bm{X}(t)$ and control variable state $\bm{u}(t)$ from feedback $\otimes$. Then, based off the structure of the cost functional $J$, the mechanism determines the optimal control update, and updates $\bm{u}(t)$ accordingly:   
