\section*{Dynamical Constraint Implementation}
In order to establish the optimized dynamics between the system's components, an intermediate state variable ought to be implemented. In particular, let the costate be denoted by $\bm{\lambda}:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^N$, and we can use it to construct the augmented cost functional $\tilde J$ by treating it as a Lagrange multiplier. Specifically, it can be implemented as such in order to restrict the system to its dynamical constraint to be extremized over:
\begin{align*}
\tilde J[\bm X,\bm u,\bm\lambda]&=J[\bm X,\bm u]-\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\bm{\lambda}(t)^T\left(\bm{\dot X}(t)-f(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))\right)dt
\\[4 mm]
&=\Phi(\bm X(t_1))+\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\left[\mathcal{L}(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))-\bm{\lambda}(t)^T\left(\bm{\dot X}(t)-f(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))\right)\right]dt
\end{align*}
With the augmented functional in place, a characteristic objective function of the system's configuration, given by the Hamiltonian will be defined by:
\[
\mathcal{H}(t,x,u,\lambda)\coloneq\mathcal{L}(t,x,u)+\lambda^Tf(t,x,u)
\]
This allows us to recast the integrand of the augmented cost functional in terms of a function of the system's configuration $\mathcal{H}(t,x,u,\lambda)$ and the state's (coupled) configuration trend $\lambda^T\dot x$ as given below:
\[
\tilde J[\bm X,\bm u,\bm{\lambda}]=\Phi(\bm X(t_1))+\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\left[\mathcal{H}(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t),\bm{\lambda}(t))-\bm{\lambda}(t)^T\bm{\dot X}(t)\right]dt
\]

\newpage

\section*{The Optimality Conditions}
We now take the variation of the cost functional $\tilde J$ with respect to each of its arguments. This will enable us to determine the extremal functions that optimize the cost. For convenience, let $\bm S(t)$ denote the system configuration $(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t),\bm{\lambda}(t))$. It follows from linearity, the chain rule, and integration by parts:
\begin{align*}
&\delta\tilde J[\bm X,\bm u,\bm{\lambda}]=\delta \Phi(\bm X(t_1))+\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\delta\left[\mathcal{H}(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t),\bm{\lambda}(t))-\bm{\lambda}(t)^T\bm{\dot X}(t)\right]dt
\\[4 mm]
&=\left.\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm X(t_1)}\delta\bm X(t_1)+\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\left[\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm S}\delta\bm X+\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial u}\right|_{\bm S}\delta\bm u+\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial\lambda}\right|_{\bm S}\delta\bm{\lambda}-\delta\bm{\lambda}^T\bm{\dot X}-\bm{\lambda}^T\delta\bm{\dot X}\right](t)\,dt
\\[4 mm]
&=\left.\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm X(t_1)}\delta\bm X(t_1)-\left.\bm{\lambda}^T\delta\bm X\right|_{\cancel{t_0}}^{t_1}\;+\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\left[\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm S}\delta\bm X+\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial u}\right|_{\bm S}\delta\bm u+\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial\lambda}\right|_{\bm S}\delta\bm{\lambda}-\bm{\dot X}^T\delta\bm{\lambda}-\bm{\dot\lambda}^T\delta\bm X\right](t)\,dt
\\[4 mm]
&=\left(\left.\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm X(t_1)}-\bm{\lambda}^T(t_1)\right)\delta\bm X(t_1)+\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\left[\left(\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm S}+\bm{\dot\lambda}^T\right)\delta\bm X+\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial u}\right|_{\bm S}\delta\bm u+\left(\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial\lambda}\right|_{\bm S}-\bm{\dot X}^T\right)\delta\bm{\lambda}\right](t)\,dt
\end{align*}
If we then make the variation in $\tilde J$ stationary by setting $\delta\tilde J=0$, because the collection $\left\{\delta\bm X,\delta\bm u,\delta\bm{\lambda}\right\}$ is linearly independent by necessity and $\delta\bm X(t_1)$ is arbitrary, whereas $\delta\bm X(t_0)=0$, the following conditions must hold simultaneously at the optimal configuration $\bm S(t)=(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t),\bm{\lambda}(t))$ for all $t\in[t_0,t_1)$:
\[
(\bm{\lambda})^T(t_1)=\left.\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm X(t_1)}
\qquad\&\qquad
(\bm{\dot\lambda})^T=-\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm S}
\qquad\&\qquad
\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial u}\right|_{\bm S}=0
\qquad\&\qquad
(\bm{\dot X})^T=\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial\lambda}\right|_{\bm S}
\]
Upon explicitly working out the partial derivatives of the Hamiltonian, the conditions in conjunction with the original initial condition reduce to the coupled system of differential equations as follows:
\[
\begin{cases}
(\bm{\dot\lambda})^T(t)=-\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial x}(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))-(\bm{\lambda})^T(t)\,\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad (\bm{\lambda})^T(t_1)=\dfrac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x}(\bm X(t_1))
\\\\
\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial u}(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))+(\bm{\lambda})^T(t)\,\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial u}(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))=0
\\\\
(\bm{\dot X})^T(t)=f^T(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad\bm X(t_0)=\bm X_0
\end{cases}
\]
With the understanding that the bold font and the (implied) function argument inputs will be dropped appropriately for convenience, these equations, which are referred to as the optimality conditions, can be further structurally manipulated as such:
\[
\begin{cases}
\dot\lambda+\left(\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)^T\lambda+\left(\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial x}\right)^T=0\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad\lambda(t_1)=\left(\dfrac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x}\right)^T(x(t_1))
\\\\
\left(\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial u}\right)^T\lambda+\left(\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial u}\right)^T=0
\\\\
\dot x=f(t,x,u)\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad x(t_0)=x_0
\end{cases}
\]
To briefly comment on the infinite horizon problem, in which $t_1\to\infty$ and $\Phi\equiv0$, as $\delta x(t_1)$ is no longer a fixed independent variable, the terminal condition on $\lambda$ is necessarily replaced by the transversal condition:
\[
\lim\limits_{t\to\infty}\lambda^T(t)\,\delta x(t)=0
\]
These coupled equations constitute the first-order necessary optimality conditions for the control problem. Unfortunately, there is no closed form analytic solution in the general case of non-linear dynamics. However, restricting the analysis to LTI systems indeed simplifies matters significantly.

