Structurally, the two inputs given by $\bm{X}(t)$ and $\bm{u}(t)$ in addition to the (initialized) costate $\bm{\lambda}(t)$ feed into the internal state machines. The state machine $-\mathcal{H}_x^T(t,x,u,\lambda)$ directly returns the costate velocity $\bm{\dot\lambda}(t)$, which is then integrated, and subsequently outputs the updated costate $\bm\lambda(t+dt)$ for feedback $\otimes$. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Likewise, the state machine $\mathcal{H}_u^T(t,x,u,\lambda)$ is then fed the costate $\bm\lambda(t)$ in addition to the inputs given by $\bm X(t)$ and $\bm u(t)$. From there, the state machine determines the (closest) new control variable state $\bm u(t+dt)$ which satisfies the equation $\mathcal{H}_u^T(t,\bm{X}(t),u,\bm\lambda(t))=0$, and can then be fed back into the feedback loop.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Crucially, this only a well posed interpretation provided that the Hessian satisfies $\det(\partial_u^2\mathcal{H})\neq0$ at the optimal configuration. This is because, noting $\partial_u\mathcal{H}=0$ per the optimality conditions, by the implicit function theorem, there necessarily exists (contextually) a smooth mapping $(t,x,\lambda)\mapsto u$ if the Hessian is locally invertible as prescribed before. 

