\section*{Initialization}
Although the feedback loop described up until this point is perfectly well defined, in practice, initializing the control algorithm is rather challenging. More specifically, the costate $\bm\lambda$ lacks an explicit initial value $\bm\lambda_0=\bm\lambda(t_0)$ despite having a terminal condition $\bm\lambda(t_1)=(\partial_x\Phi)^T(\bm X(t_1)$ for finite time problems. In order to initialize the feedback loop, $\bm\lambda_0$ needs to be determined. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Consider the functions $\bm{\tilde X}$ and $\bm{\tilde\lambda}$ that takes a candidate $\lambda_0\in\mathbb{R}^N$ as input in conjunction with $\bm X_0$ to initialize the feedback loop, and returns the respective forwardly integrated variables from time $t_0$ to $t_1$:
\[
\bm{\tilde X}:\lambda_0\in\mathbb{R}^N\mapsto\bm{\tilde X}(\lambda_0)\in\mathbb{R}^N
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\bm{\tilde\lambda}:\lambda_0\in\mathbb{R}^N\mapsto\bm{\tilde\lambda}(\lambda_0)\in\mathbb{R}^N
\]
Then, consider the objective function $F$, which accepts the guessed costate $\lambda_0$ as an input, and calculates the error between the corresponding terminal costate and the targeted terminal costate:
\[
F: \lambda_0\in\mathbb{R}^N\mapsto\bm{\tilde\lambda}(\lambda_0)-\left(\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x}\right)^T\left(\bm{\tilde X}(\lambda_0)\right)
\]
At this point, the problem of determining $\bm\lambda_0$ has been reduced to the root finding problem $F(\bm\lambda_0)=0$, in which there exist several numerical solving techniques (e.g. Newton-Raphson). Note that this strictly applies to the finite time case. For infinite horizon problems, one needs to determine/approximate the targeted terminal costate, which is a process beyond the scope of these notes.

