\section*{System Valuation Evolution}
Instead of analyzing the system's trajectory over its entire time history, we now consider how it behaves from some intermediate time $t$ to the final time $t_1$. More specifically, given that the system's dynamics are deterministic, fixing the configuration at any time determines the state's (optimal) future and past. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
In this light, given an intermediate state configuration pairing $(t,\bm X_t)$, there exists a unique function of the form $\xi:\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^N\to\mathbb{R}^N$ such that at any time $\tau$ one has:
\[
\bm X(\tau)=\xi(\tau,t,\bm X_t)
\quad\iff\quad
\bm{\dot X}(\tau)=f(\tau,\bm X(\tau),\bm u(\tau))
\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad
\bm X(t)=\bm X_t
\]
Casting the state evolution in this manner allows for the construction of a value function $V$ called the valuation. It determines the accumulated optimal cost between the current configuration $(t,\bm X_t)$ up to the final configuration $(t_1,\bm X(t_1))$. In the established context, the valuation takes the following form:
\[
V(t,\bm X_t)=\Phi(\xi(t_1,t,\bm X_t))+\int_t^{t_1}\mathcal{L}\left(\tau,\xi(\tau,t,\bm X_t),\bm u(\tau)\right)d\tau
\]
Carefully applying the chain rule in conjunction with the Leibnitz rule for integration results in the following equality which describes how the valuation changes:
\begin{align*}
&\frac{d}{dt}V(t,\bm X(t))=\frac{\partial V}{\partial t}(t,\bm X(t))+\frac{\partial V}{\partial x}(t,\bm X(t))\,\bm{\dot X}(t)=\frac{\partial V}{\partial t}(t,\bm X(t))+\frac{\partial V}{\partial x}(t,\bm X(t))\,f(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))
\\[4 mm]
&=\underbrace{\frac{d}{dt}\Phi(\xi(t_1,t,\bm X(t)))}_{=0}+\frac{d}{dt}\int_t^{t_1}\mathcal{L}\left(\tau,\xi(\tau,t,\bm X(\tau)),\bm u(\tau)\right)d\tau\qquad\text{since}\qquad\frac{d}{dt}\xi(t_1,t,\bm X(t))=0
\\[2 mm]
&=-\mathcal{L}(t,\xi(t,t,\bm X(t)),\bm u(t))+\int_t^{t_1}\underbrace{\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\left[\mathcal{L}\left(\tau,\xi(\tau,t,\bm X(t)),\bm u(\tau)\right)\right]}_{=0}d\tau\qquad\text{since}\qquad\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\left[\xi(\tau,t,\bm X(t))\right]=0
\\[2 mm]
&=-\mathcal{L}(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))\qquad\text{since}\qquad\xi(t,t,\bm X(t))=\bm X(t)
\end{align*}
This culminates in a relationship between how the valuation $V$ evolves explicitly and the running cost $\mathcal{L}$ of the optimal system. Noting the absence of the terminal function $\Phi$, it follows:
\[
\frac{\partial V}{\partial t}(t,\bm X(t))+\frac{\partial V}{\partial x}(t,\bm X(t))\,f(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))+\mathcal{L}(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))=0
\]

\section*{Costate Origins}
In order to uncover the relationship between the valuation $V$ and the costate $\bm\lambda$, we consider the first order variation in the valuation itself at an arbitrary state:
\begin{align*}
&\delta V(t,\bm X(t))=\frac{\partial V}{\partial x}(t,\bm X(t))\,\delta\bm X(t)
\\[4 mm]
&=\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x}(\bm X(t_1))\,\delta\bm X(t_1)+\int_t^{t_1}\left[\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial x}(\tau,\xi(\tau,t,\bm X(t)),\bm u(\tau))\,\delta\xi(\tau,t,\bm X(t))+\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial u}(\tau,\xi(\tau,t,\bm X(t)),\bm u(\tau))\,\delta\bm u(\tau)\right]d\tau
\\[4 mm]
&=\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x}(\bm X(t_1))\,\delta\bm X(t_1)+\int_t^{t_1}\left[\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial x}(\tau,\bm X(\tau),\bm u(\tau))\,\delta\bm X(\tau)+\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial u}(\tau,\bm X(\tau),\bm u(\tau))\,\delta\bm u(\tau)\right]d\tau
\end{align*}
Crucially, the variations $\delta\bm X$ and $\delta\bm u$ are not independent. This is due to the dynamical constraint, which we recall as $\bm{\dot X}(\tau)=f(\tau,\bm X(\tau),\bm u(\tau))$, in variation form provided as such:
\[
\delta\bm{\dot X}(\tau)=\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm I(\tau)}\delta\bm X(\tau)+\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial u}\right|_{\bm I(\tau)}\delta\bm u(\tau)
\qquad\text{given the input}\qquad
\bm I(\tau)=(\tau,\bm X(\tau),\bm u(\tau))
\]

\newpage

Therefore, upon introducing the Lagrange Multiplier $\bm\lambda$ (i.e. the costate), the following integral must vanish per the dynamic variational constraint:
\[
\int_t^{t_1}\bm\lambda^T(\tau)\left[\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm I}\delta\bm X+\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial u}\right|_{\bm I}\delta\bm u-\delta\bm{\dot X}\right](\tau)\,d\tau=0
\]
This fact in conjunction with the optimality conditions can be combined in order to simplify the variation in the configured valuation functional $\delta V(t,\bm X(t))$ (with integration by parts) as shown below:
\begin{align*}
    &\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x}(\bm X(t_1))\,\delta\bm X(t_1)+\int_t^{t_1}\left[\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm I}\delta\bm X+\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial u}\right|_{\bm I}\delta\bm u\right](\tau)\,d\tau+\int_t^{t_1}\bm\lambda^T(\tau)\left[\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm I}\delta\bm X+\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial u}\right|_{\bm I}\delta\bm u-\delta\bm{\dot X}\right](\tau)\,d\tau
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x}(\bm X(t_1))\,\delta\bm X(t_1)+\int_t^{t_1}\left[\left(\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm I}+\bm\lambda^T\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm I}\right)\delta\bm X+\left(\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial u}\right|_{\bm I}+\bm\lambda^T\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial u}\right|_{\bm I}\right)\delta\bm u-\bm\lambda^T\delta\bm{\dot X}\right](\tau)\,d\tau
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\left[\left.\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm X}\delta\bm X\right](t_1)-\left.\bm\lambda^T\delta\bm X\right|_t^{t_1}+\int_t^{t_1}\left[\left(\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm I}+\bm\lambda^T\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm I}+\bm{\dot\lambda}^T\right)\delta\bm X+\left(\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial u}\right|_{\bm I}+\bm\lambda^T\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial u}\right|_{\bm I}\right)\delta\bm u\right](\tau)\,d\tau
    \\[4 mm]
    &=\bm\lambda^T(t)\,\delta\bm X(t)+\underbrace{\left(\left.\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm X(t_1)}-\bm\lambda^T(t_1)\right)\delta\bm X(t_1)}_{=0}+\int_t^{t_1}\Bigg[\underbrace{\left(\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial x}\right|_{\bm S}+\bm{\dot\lambda}^T\right)}_{=0}\delta\bm X+\underbrace{\left.\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial u}\right|_{\bm S}}_{=0}\delta\bm u\Bigg](\tau)\,d\tau
\end{align*}
Once the optimality conditions are applied, the only term that survives the cancellations is $\bm\lambda^T(t)\,\delta\bm X(t)$. Recall though that $\delta V(t,\bm X(t))$ is also equivalent to $\partial_x V(t,\bm X(t))\,\delta\bm X(t)$, and so because $\delta\bm X(t)$ is arbitrary such that $t\neq t_0$, the following relation can be established:
\[
\bm\lambda^T(t)\,\delta\bm X(t)=\frac{\partial V}{\partial x}(t,\bm X(t))\,\delta\bm X(t)
\quad\implies\quad
\bm\lambda^T(t)=\frac{\partial V}{\partial x}(t,\bm X(t))
\quad\iff\quad
\bm\lambda(t)=\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial x}\right)^T(t,\bm X(t))
\]
This is all to say, the costate $\bm\lambda$ is determined by the value function $V$. In fact, with sufficient information, the costate can be initialized by $\bm\lambda_0=(\partial_xV)^T(t_0,\bm X_0)$. Moreover, inserting this result into the relationship between the valuation and the running cost:
\[
\frac{\partial V}{\partial t}(t,\bm X(t))+\bm\lambda^T(t)\,f(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))+\mathcal{L}(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t))=0
\]
Upon further inspection, by definition, the above equation can be casted in terms the following compact relationship between the value function $V$ and the Hamiltonian $\mathcal{H}$ given as such:
\[
\frac{\partial V}{\partial t}(t,\bm X(t))+\mathcal{H}(t,\bm X(t),\bm u(t),\bm\lambda (t))=0
\]
Upon noting $\partial_u\mathcal{H}=0$, it follows that $\mathcal{H}$ attains a minimum, maximum, or a saddle point. However, in order to minimize the remaining cost $V$, the optimal controller $\bm u$ must minimize the Hamiltonian $\mathcal{H}$ over some support $\mathcal{U}$. This is encapsulated by the Hamilton Jacobi Bellman Equation (HJB) as below:
\[
\boxed{\;
\frac{\partial V}{\partial t}(t,\bm X(t))+\min_{u\,\in\,\mathcal{U}}\mathcal{H}(t,\bm X(t),u,\bm\lambda (t))=0\vphantom{\frac{\frac{1}{1}}{\frac{1}{1}}}
\;}
\]
Alternatively, this condition encoded by the HJB is by invoking Pontryagin's Minimum Principle (PMP) in context, which actually is an equivalent form of the optimality conditions, and is given here:
\[
\boxed{\;
\bm u(t)=\underset{u\,\in\,\mathcal{U}}{\operatorname{argmin}}\,\mathcal{H}(t,\bm X(t),u,\bm\lambda (t))\vphantom{\frac{a}{b}}
\;}
\]

