\section*{The Linear Quadratic Regulator}
Within the established context up until this point, suppose that the dynamics of the system is LTI. In particular, given matrices $A\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}$ and $B\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times m}$, one has a system in state-space:
\[
\bm{\dot X}=A\bm X+B\bm u\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad\bm X(0)=\bm X_0
\]
Likewise, provided a positive semi-definite (i.e. $\succeq0$) matrix $Q\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}$ and a positive definite (i.e. $\succ0$) matrix $R\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}$ (both symmetric), we can construct a cost functional in accordance with:
\[
J[\bm X,\bm u]=\int_0^\infty\left(\bm X^T(t)Q\bm X(t)+\bm u^T(t)R\bm u(t)\right)dt
\]
Neglecting the terminal cost $\Phi$, which is null regardless, we can identify the $f$ and $\mathcal{L}$ functions corresponding to this infinite horizon problem as follows:
\[
f(x,u)=Ax+Bu\qquad\text{and}\qquad\mathcal{L}(x,u)=x^TQx+u^TRu
\]
Such a control system is referred to as a linear quadratic regulator (LQR). We now move on to compute the necessary partial derivatives in order to simplify the optimality conditions as such: 
\begin{align*}
    \left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial x}\right)^T&=\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(x^TQx+\cancel{u^TRu}\right)\right)^T=\left(\frac{\partial x^T}{\partial x}Qx+x^TQ\frac{\partial x}{\partial x}\right)^T=\left((Qx)^T+x^TQ\right)^T=\left(Q+Q^T\right)x=2Qx
    \\[4 mm]
    \left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial u}\right)^T&=\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial u}\left(\cancel{x^TQx}+u^TRu\right)\right)^T=\left(\frac{\partial u^T}{\partial u}Ru+u^TR\frac{\partial u}{\partial u}\right)^T=\left((Ru)^T+u^TR\right)^T=\left(R+R^T\right)u=2Ru
    \\[4 mm]
    \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)^T&=\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(Ax+\cancel{Bu}\right)\right)^T=\left(A\frac{\partial x}{\partial x}\right)^T=A^T
    \\[4 mm]
    \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial u}\right)^T&=\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial u}\left(\cancel{Ax}+Bu\right)\right)^T=\left(B\frac{\partial u}{\partial u}\right)^T=B^T
\end{align*}
Substituting these expressions, which were derived from the LQR model, into the previously developed optimality conditions results in the following system of ordinary differential equations (ODEs):
\[
\begin{cases}
    \dot\lambda+A^T\lambda+2Qx=0\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad\lambda^T\delta x\to0
    \\\\
    B^T\lambda+2Ru=0
    \\\\
    \dot x=Ax+Bu\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad x(0)=x_0
\end{cases}
\]
Since $\mathcal{L}$ is a (positive) quadratic form, we expect there to be an optimal configuration $(x,u)$ determined by the above system of ODEs that minimizes $J$ subject to the dynamical constraint because $J$ is convex. Now, consider the Hamiltonian corresponding to the LQR system:
\[
\mathcal{H}(t,x,u,\lambda)=x^TQx+u^TRu+\lambda^T(Ax+Bu)
\]
More specifically, observe the behavior of the respective Jacobian and Hessian pertaining to the Hamiltonian when subject to the governing optimality conditions:
\[
\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial u}=0
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\frac{\partial^2\mathcal{H}}{\partial u^2}=\lambda^T\,\frac{\partial^2f}{\partial u^2}+\frac{\partial^2\mathcal{L}}{\partial u^2}=2R\succ0
\]
Because the Jacobian is null and the Hessian is positive definite, the Hamiltonian necessarily attains a minimum when subject to the optimality conditions (pure state feedback law). That is, the control $\bm{u}$ that minimizes $\mathcal{H}$ is the control that minimizes $J$ (due to convexity) subject to the dynamical constraint.

\section*{The Algebraic Riccati Equation}
Now that the governing optimality conditions have been determined for the LQR model, as the respective system of equations are linear ODEs with constant coefficients, from the theory of differential equations, there necessarily exists a unique solution.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Because of the existence of such a solution, an ansatz will be made in order to solve the system. In particular, we will assume the costate is strictly determined by the state variable:
\[
\lambda=2Px\qquad\text{for some}\qquad P\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times  N}
\]
Directly substituting the ansatz into the ODEs given by the optimality conditions results in the following:
\[
\begin{cases}
    2P\dot x+2A^TPx+2Qx=0\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad2x^TP^T\delta x\to0
    \\\\
    2B^TPx+2Ru=0
    \\\\
    \dot x=Ax+Bu\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad x(0)=x_0
\end{cases}
\]
Upon simplifying the current system of equations, whilst noting that $R$ is invertible as because $R\succ0$, the control variable $u$ can be isolated as such:
\[
\begin{cases}
    P\dot x+\left(A^TP+Q\right)x=0\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad x^TP^T\delta x\to0
    \\\\
    u=-R^{-1}B^TPx
    \\\\
    \dot x=Ax+Bu\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad x(0)=x_0
\end{cases}
\]
At this point, the (isolated) expressions for $u$ and $\dot x$ can be substituted into the costate (upper) equation in order to arrive at a purely algebraic equation:
\[
P\left(Ax+B\left(-R^{-1}B^TPx\right)\right)+\left(A^TP+Q\right)x=0
\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad
x^TP^T\delta x\to0
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
x(0)=x_0
\]
Moreover, the system can be further simplified by factoring out the variable $x$ from the algebraic equation as follows in order to determine the system's homogeneous conditions:
\[
\left(PA-PBR^{-1}B^TP+A^TP+Q\right)x=0
\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad
x^TP^T\delta x\to0
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
x(0)=x_0
\]
Under the assumption that the traversal condition holds and that the state is initialized accordingly, because the above algebraic equation holds for every state configuration $x$, the respective matrix must therefore be zero as it is constant in $\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}$. This is precisely the Algebraic Riccati Equation (ARE):
\[
\boxed{\;
PA-PBR^{-1}B^TP+A^TP+Q=0\vphantom{\frac{a}{b}}
\;}
\qquad\text{with}\qquad
\boxed{\;
u=-Kx\vphantom{\frac{a}{b}}
\quad\text{s.t.}\quad
K=R^{-1}B^TP
\;}
\]
The system thus obeys a pure state feedback law, and the controller can be characterizes by a gain matrix $K$ as shown above. Furthermore, the system as a whole can be characterized by the following diagram:

