\section*{LQR Equilibrium Solutions}
Assuming controllability, upon inspecting the solution to the LQR problem, the state variable $\bm X$ must evolve according to the following equations in order to meet the optimality conditions:
\[
\bm{\dot X}=(A-BK)\bm X
\quad\implies\quad
\bm X(t)=e^{(A-BK)t}\bm X_0
\]
If all of the Eigenvalues of $A-BK$ are (real part) negative, then the state variable $\bm X$ must decay to zero. Otherwise, $\bm X$ oscillates, is constant, or $||\bm X||\to\infty$. Before advancing, we recast the ARE in terms of $K$:
\begin{align*}
    &PA-PBR^{-1}B^TP+A^TP+Q=PA-PBK+A^TP+Q
    \\[4 mm]
    &=P(A-BK)+\left(A^T-(BK)^T\right)P+(BK)^TP+Q=P(A-BK)+(A-BK)^TP+K^TB^TP+Q
    \\[4 mm]
    &=P(A-BK)+(A-BK)^TP+K^TRK+Q=0
\end{align*}
Now, let $\bm v$ be an Eigenvector of $A-BK$ with a corresponding Eigenvalue $\mu$. Taking into consideration the conjugate transpose operation, we thus obtain the following equations given below:
\[
\left(A-BK\right)\bm v=\mu\bm v\quad\iff\quad\bm v^*\left(A-BK\right)^T=\mu^*\bm v^*
\]
Sandwiching the ARE in between the Eigenvector $\bm v$  and its conjugate transpose $\bm v^*$ thereby results in the relationship over the scalar field $\mathbb{C}$ as follows:
\begin{align*}
&\bm v^*\left(P(A-BK)+(A-BK)^TP+K^TRK+Q\right)\bm v
\\[4 mm]
&=\bm v^*P\left(A-BK\right)\bm v+\bm v^*\left(A-BK\right)^TP\bm v+\bm v^*K^TRK\bm v+\bm v^*Q\bm v
\\[4 mm]
&=\bm v^*P\mu\bm v+\mu^*\bm v^*P\bm v+\bm v^*K^TRK\bm v+\bm v^*Q\bm v
\\[4 mm]
&=(\mu+\mu^*)\bm v^*P\bm v+\bm v^*K^TRK\bm v+\bm v^*Q\bm v
\\[4 mm]
&=\underbrace{2\mathrm{Re}(\mu)\bm v^*P\bm v}_{<0}+\underbrace{(K\bm v)^*R\,(K\bm v)}_{>0}+\underbrace{\bm v^*Q\bm v}_{\geq0}=\bm v^*(0)\bm v=0
\qquad\text{since}\qquad R\succ0\qquad\text{and}\qquad Q\succeq0
\end{align*}
In order to deduce the sign of $\mathrm{Re}(\mu)$, we must determine the sign of $\bm v^*P\bm v$. That is, we must determine if $P\succeq0$. This will be done by plugging the solution into the value function $V\in\mathbb{R}_0^+$, noting that $\bm u=-K\bm X$:
\begin{align*}
&V(t,\bm X(t))=\int_t^\infty\left[\bm X^TQ\bm X+\bm u^TR\bm u\right](\tau)\,d\tau+\underbrace{\int_t^\infty\frac{d}{d\tau}\left[\bm X^TP\bm X\right](\tau)\,d\tau-\int_t^\infty\frac{d}{d\tau}\left[\bm X^TP\bm X\right](\tau)\,d\tau}_{=0}
\\[4 mm]
&=\int_t^\infty\Big[\bm X^TQ\bm X+\bm u^TR\bm u+\underbrace{\bm{\dot X}^TP\bm X+\bm X^TP\bm{\dot X}}_{\frac{d}{d\tau}\left[\bm X^TP\bm X\right]}\Big](\tau)\,dt-\left.\bm X^TP\bm X\right|_t^\infty
\\[4 mm]
&=\int_t^\infty\left[\bm X^TQ\bm X+(-K\bm X)^TR\,(-K\bm X)+\left((A-BK)\bm X\right)^TP\bm X+\bm X^TP\left((A-BK)\bm X\right)\right](\tau)\,d\tau-\left.\bm X^TP\bm X\right|_t^\infty
\\[4 mm]
&=\int_t^\infty\bm X^T(\tau)\big[\underbrace{\left(PA-PBK+A^TP+Q\right)}_{=0}+K^TR\underbrace{\left(K-R^{-1}B^TP\right)}_{=0}\big]\bm X(\tau)\,d\tau-\left.\bm X^TP\bm X\right|_t^\infty
\\[4 mm]
&=\bm X^T(t)P\bm X(t)-\lim_{t\to\infty}\bm X^T(t)P\bm X(t)\geq0
\quad\implies\quad 
\bm X^T(t)P\bm X(t)\geq\lim_{t\to\infty}\bm X^T(t)P\bm X(t)
\end{align*}
Upon setting $t=0$, because $P$ does not depend on $\bm X_0$ in the ARE, varying $\bm X_0$ does not alter $P$. In this regard, without loss of generality, choose $\bm X_0$ such that $\bm X_0^TP\bm X_0=2\lim\limits_{t\to\infty}\bm X^T(t)P\bm X(t)$, which implies:
\[
\bm X^T_0P\bm X_0-\lim\limits_{t\to\infty}\bm X^T(t)P\bm X(t)=\lim\limits_{t\to\infty}\bm X^T(t)P\bm X(t)\geq0
\quad\implies\quad
\bm X^T(t)P\bm X(t)\geq0
\]. This is to say, $P\succeq0$.

Due to the fact that $\mathrm{Re}(\mu)\bm v^*P\bm v<0$, by the zero product law, $\bm v^*P\bm v\neq0$. Therefore, as $P\succeq0$, one has $\bm v^*P\bm v>0$, and so $\mathrm{Re}(\mu)<0$, which implies the state $\bm X$ must decay to zero regardless of the initial condition:
\[
\boxed{\;
\lim_{t\to\infty}\bm X(t)=\lim_{t\to\infty}e^{(A-BK)t}\bm X_0=0\vphantom{\frac{a}{b}}
\;}
\]
This directly validates the transversal condition $\bm X^TP^T\delta\bm X\to0$. Moreover, the minimal valuation/cost associated with the optimized control setup can be calculated and characterized accordingly:
\[
\boxed{\;
V(t,\bm X(t))=\bm X^T(t)P\bm X(t)\vphantom{\frac{a}{b}}
\;}
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\boxed{\;
J[\bm X,\bm u]=V(0,\bm X_0)=\bm X_0^TP\bm X_0\vphantom{\frac{a}{b}}
\;}
\]
\end{comment}


\section*{Optimal Linearization}
Let us reconsider the general time invariant state evolution equation pertaining to the sate variable $\bm X$ with an admissible control $\bm u\in\mathcal{U}$, where $\bm{\dot X}(t)=f(\bm X(t),\bm u(t))$ such that $\bm X(0)=\bm X_0$. Furthermore,  assume that the system is controllable within some connected region in state space containing $\bm X_0,\bm{\bar X}\in\mathbb{R}^N$.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Given such an operating state $\bm{\bar X}$, it is desired to find an optimal control variable $\bm u$ that forces $\bm{\bar X}$ to be an equilibrium state of the system (i.e. $\bm X\to\bm{\bar X}$). To do this, we consider a controller of the form:
\[
\bm u(t)=\bm{\bar u}+\delta\bm u(t)
\qquad\text{for some}\qquad
\bm{\bar u}\in\mathbb{R}^N
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\delta\bm u:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^m
\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad
\bm{\dot{\!\bar{X}}}\coloneq f\left(\bm{\bar X},\bm{\bar u}\right)=0
\]
Likewise, we consider perturbing the state $\bm X$ from the operating state $\bm{\bar X}$ in the same manner:
\[
\bm X(t)=\bm{\bar X}+\delta\bm X(t)
\qquad\text{for some}\qquad
\delta\bm X:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^N
\]
Finally, we consider perturbing the state velocity by analyzing the system locally around the equilibrium or operating point $(\bm{\bar X},\bm{\bar u})$ as such:
\begin{align*}
\delta\bm{\dot X}(t)\coloneq\bm{\dot X}(t)-\bm{\dot{\!\bar{X}}}&=f\left(\bm{\bar X}+\delta\bm X(t),\bm{\bar u}+\delta\bm u(t)\right)-\cancel{f\left(\bm{\bar X},\bm{\bar u}\right)}
\\[4 mm]
&=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\left(\bm{\bar X},\bm{\bar u}\right)\delta\bm X(t)+\frac{\partial f}{\partial u}\left(\bm{\bar X},\bm{\bar u}\right)\delta\bm u(t)+\mathcal{O}\left(||[\delta\bm X(t),\delta\bm u(t)]||^2\right)
\end{align*}
We can now treat $\delta\bm X$ and $\delta\bm u$ as auxiliary state and controller variables respectively pertaining to the system. If not clear, the initial condition associated with this auxiliary state variable is $\delta\bm X(0)=\delta\bm X_0=\bm X_0-\bm{\bar X}$. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Moreover, for sufficiently \emph{small} $\delta\bm X$ and $\delta\bm u$, the system can be considered LTI locally around the operating point $(\bm{\bar X},\bm{\bar u})$. Therefore, upon implementing a quadratic regulator/running cost function, the system can be optimized via the LQR algorithm. Firstly, we extract the corresponding $A$ and $B$ matrices:
\[
A=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\left(\bm{\bar X},\bm{\bar u}\right)
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
B=\frac{\partial f}{\partial u}\left(\bm{\bar X},\bm{\bar u}\right)
\]
Then, for some corresponding $Q$ and $R$ matrices associated with the implicit cost functional, we obtain the ARE in conjunction with the auxiliary control law:
\[
PA-PBR^{-1}B^TP+A^TP+Q=0
\qquad\text{with}\qquad
\delta\bm u\approx-K\delta\bm X
\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad
K=R^{-1}B^TP
\]
Lastly, substituting the definitions for $\delta\bm X$ and $\delta\bm u$ into the above auxiliary optimal conditions results in the locally optimal control law:
\[
\boxed{\;
\bm u(t)\approx\bm{\bar u}-K\left(\bm X(t)-\bm{\bar X}\right)\vphantom{\frac{\frac{a}{b}}{\frac{a}{b}}}
\;}
\]
If there exists a $\bm{\bar u}$ such that $f(\bm{\bar X},\bm{\bar u})=0$, then the above control law is guaranteed to force the system to reach equilibrium around the operating state $\bm{\bar X}$ for a sufficiently \emph{close} $\bm X_0$ as $\delta\bm X=\bm X-\bm{\bar X}\to0$. Importantly, if $\bm X_0$ is sufficiently \emph{far} from the operating state $\bm{\bar X}$, then the state may not reach equilibrium around $\bm{\bar X}$.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
As a side note:
\[
\boxed{\;
V(t,\bm X(t))=\bm X^T(t)P\bm X(t)\vphantom{\frac{a}{b}}
\;}
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\boxed{\;
J[\bm X,\bm u]=V(0,\bm X_0)=\bm X_0^TP\bm X_0\vphantom{\frac{a}{b}}
\;}
\]
Under the assumption:
\[
\boxed{\;
\lim_{t\to\infty}\bm X(t)=\lim_{t\to\infty}e^{(A-BK)t}\bm X_0=0\vphantom{\frac{a}{b}}
\;}
\]
\newpage

