In this illustration, a vertical pendulum of length $\ell$ with a point mass $m$, which makes an angle $\theta$ with the vertical, is attached to a platform with wheels of radius $r$ mounted to it. The wheels spin with a controllable angular velocity $\omega$ under the no slip condition. Given a uniform gravitational field $g$, the Lagrangian of the system (not to be confused with the running cost function), can be given as such:
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{L}&=\underbrace{\tfrac{1}{2}m\left|\left|(r\omega,0)+\tfrac{d}{dt}(\ell\sin\theta,\ell\cos\theta)\right|\right|^2}_{\text{Kinetic}}-\underbrace{mg\ell\cos\theta\vphantom{\tfrac{1}{2}}}_{\text{Potential}}=\tfrac{1}{2}m\left|\left|\left(r\omega+\ell\dot\theta\cos\theta,-\ell\dot\theta\sin\theta\right)\right|\right|^2-mg\ell\cos\theta
\\[4 mm]
&=\tfrac{1}{2}m\left((r\omega+\ell\dot\theta\cos\theta)^2+(-\ell\dot\theta\sin\theta)^2\right)-mg\ell\cos\theta=\tfrac{1}{2}mr^2\omega^2+\tfrac{1}{2}m\ell^2\dot\theta^2+mr\omega\ell\dot\theta\cos\theta-mg\ell\cos\theta
\end{align*}
With the Lagrangian $\mathcal{L}$ at hand, the conjugate (angular) momentum and force (torque) associated with the system can be determined as follows:
\begin{align*}
\text{Conjugate Momentum:}\quad&\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\dot\theta}=m\ell^2\dot\theta+mr\omega\ell\cos\theta\quad\implies\quad
\frac{d}{dt}\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\dot\theta}=m\ell^2\ddot\theta+mr\dot\omega\ell\cos\theta-mr\omega\ell\dot\theta\sin\theta
\\[4 mm]
\text{Conjugate Force:}\quad&\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\theta}=-mr\omega\ell\dot\theta\sin\theta+mg\ell\sin\theta
\end{align*}
According to the Euler-Lagrange Equation (i.e. Newton's Second Law), the equation of motion pertaining to the angle made with the vertical $\theta$ is provided as such:
\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dt}\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\dot\theta}=\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\theta}
\quad&\implies\quad
m\ell^2\ddot\theta+mr\dot\omega\ell\cos\theta-\cancel{mr\omega\ell\dot\theta\sin\theta}=-\cancel{mr\omega\ell\dot\theta\sin\theta}+mg\ell\sin\theta
\\[4 mm]
&\implies\quad
\boxed{\;
\ell\ddot\theta+r\dot\omega\cos\theta=g\sin\theta\vphantom{\frac{a}{b}}
\;}
\end{align*}
Keeping in mind that $[\theta,\dot\theta]^T$ is the state variable and $\dot\omega$ is the effective control variable, the equation of motion can be written in state-space form as below:
\[
\frac{d}{dt}
\begin{bmatrix}
    \,\theta\,\\\,\dot\theta\,
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
    \dot\theta\\\;\frac{g}{\ell}\sin\theta-\frac{r}{\ell}\dot\omega\cos\theta\;
\end{bmatrix}
\quad\implies\quad
\left.\frac{d}{dt}
\begin{bmatrix}
    \,\theta\,\\\,\dot\theta\,
\end{bmatrix}
\right|_{(\theta,\dot\theta,\dot\omega)=(0,0,0)}
=\begin{bmatrix}
    \,0\,\\\,0\,
\end{bmatrix}
\quad\text{corresponding to an equilibrium}
\]
By inspection, although this system is time invariant, it is not linear in the state variable (specifically $\theta$), and so the system is not LTI. In this respect, determining an analytical model for an optimal control law would be a formidable challenge, and will not be entertained.

\newpage

In order to simplify matters, the state equation will be linearized around the operating (equilibrium) point given by $(\theta,\dot\theta,\dot\omega)=(0,0,0)$. This of course corresponds to modeling the system locally around the upright pendulum position (i.e. $\theta=0$):
\begin{align*}
    \theta\;\text{linearization:}\quad&\left.\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \dot\theta\\\;\frac{g}{\ell}\sin\theta-\frac{r}{\ell}\dot\omega\cos\theta\;
\end{bmatrix}\right|_{(\theta,\dot\theta,\dot\omega)=(0,0,0)}
=
    \left.
    \begin{bmatrix}
    0\\\;\frac{g}{\ell}\cos\theta\;
\end{bmatrix}\right|_{\theta=0}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
    0\\\;g/\ell\;
\end{bmatrix}
\\[4 mm]
\dot\theta\;\text{linearization:}\quad&\left.\frac{\partial}{\partial\dot\theta}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \dot\theta\\\;\frac{g}{\ell}\sin\theta-\frac{r}{\ell}\dot\omega\cos\theta\;
\end{bmatrix}\right|_{(\theta,\dot\theta,\dot\omega)=(0,0,0)}
=
    \begin{bmatrix}
    1\\\;0\;
\end{bmatrix}
\\[4 mm]
\dot\omega\;\text{linearization:}\quad&\left.\frac{\partial}{\partial\dot\omega}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \dot\theta\\\;\frac{g}{\ell}\sin\theta-\frac{r}{\ell}\dot\omega\cos\theta\;
\end{bmatrix}\right|_{(\theta,\dot\theta,\dot\omega)=(0,0,0)}
=
    \left.
    \begin{bmatrix}
    0\\\;-\frac{r}{\ell}\cos\theta\;
\end{bmatrix}\right|_{\theta=0}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
    0\\\;-r/\ell\;
\end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}
With the dynamics now linearized, the state equation can be appropriately approximated by the above linearization scheme as follows noting $\delta\theta=\theta$, $\delta\dot\theta=\dot\theta,$ and $\delta\dot\omega=\dot\omega$:
\[
\frac{d}{dt}
\begin{bmatrix}
    \,\theta\,\\\,\dot\theta\,
\end{bmatrix}
\approx
\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}
    \;0 & 1\; \\
    \;g/\ell & 0\;
\end{bmatrix}}_{A}
\begin{bmatrix}
    \,\theta\,\\\,\dot\theta\,
\end{bmatrix}
+
\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}
    0\\\;-r/\ell\;
\end{bmatrix}}_{B}
\,\dot\omega
\qquad\text{for}\qquad
(\theta,\dot\theta)\approx(0,0)
\]
At this point, the Kalman Controllability Criterion ought to be checked in order to determine if an admissible control $\dot\omega$ can be effectively implemented. Per the criterion, if the rank of the following matrix is the dimension of state-space (i.e. 2), then the linearized system is indeed controllable:
\[
\mathrm{rank}\begin{bmatrix}
    \;B & AB\;
\end{bmatrix}
=
\mathrm{rank}\begin{bmatrix}
\;\begin{bmatrix}
    0\\\;-r/\ell\;
\end{bmatrix} 
&
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;0 & 1\; \\
    \;g/\ell & 0\;
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
    0\\\;-r/\ell\;
\end{bmatrix}\;
\end{bmatrix}
=
\mathrm{rank}\begin{bmatrix}
\;0 & -r/\ell\;\\
\;-r/\ell & 0\;
\end{bmatrix}
=2\;\checkmark
\]
Because the linearized system is controllable, there exists an implementable LQR control law. We now construct a cost functional $J$ that accounts for the (squared) accumulated state-equilibrium error and the corresponding control effort. In particular, given $q_{11},q_{22}\geq0$ and $R>0$, it has the following form:
\[
J[\theta,\dot\theta,\dot\omega]=\int_0^\infty\left[q_{11}\theta^2+q_{22}\dot\theta^2+R\dot\omega^2\right](t)\,dt=\int_0^\infty\left[
\begin{bmatrix}
    \,\theta &\dot\theta\,
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;q_{11} & 0\;\\
    \;0 & q_{22}\;
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
    \,\theta\,\\\,\dot\theta\,
\end{bmatrix}
+\dot\omega R\dot\omega
\right](t)\,dt
\]
The corresponding $Q$ and $R$ matrices can be identified, and so the optimal LQR control law can be determined by solving the ARE, given by $PA+A^TP-PBR^{-1}B^TP+Q=0$, for $P=[p_{ij}]_{1\leq i,j\leq2}$ as follows:
\begin{align*}
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;p_{11} & p_{12}\;\\
    \;p_{21} & p_{22}\;
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;0 & 1\;\\
    \;g/\ell & 0\;
\end{bmatrix}
&+
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;0 & g/\ell\;\\
    \;1 & 0\;
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;p_{11} & p_{12}\;\\
    \;p_{21} & p_{22}\;
\end{bmatrix}
\\[4 mm]
&-
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;p_{11} & p_{12}\;\\
    \;p_{21} & p_{22}\;
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;0\;\\
    \;-r/\ell\;
\end{bmatrix}
R^{-1}
\begin{bmatrix}
    \,0 & -r/\ell\,\,
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;p_{11} & p_{12}\;\\
    \;p_{21} & p_{22}\;
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;q_{11} & 0\;\\
    \;0 & q_{22}\;
\end{bmatrix}
=0
\end{align*}
Solving this equation is left as an exercise. It can be solved using standard algebraic techniques or by utilizing computer algebra software. In most practical settings, the ARE is solved numerically due to its inherent complexity. In any case, the solution is shown below:
\[
P=
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;\dfrac{1}{r}
    \sqrt{
    \left(g^2+\dfrac{r^2q_{11}}{R}\right)
    \left[
    Rq_{22}
    +\dfrac{2\ell R^2}{r^2}
    \left(
    g+\sqrt{g^2+\dfrac{r^2q_{11}}{R}}
    \right)
    \right]
    }
    &
    \dfrac{\ell R}{r^2}
    \left(
    g+\sqrt{g^2+\dfrac{r^2q_{11}}{R}}
    \right)\;\\[20pt]

    \;\dfrac{\ell R}{r^2}
    \left(
    g+\sqrt{g^2+\dfrac{r^2q_{11}}{R}}
    \right)
    &
    \dfrac{\ell}{r}
    \sqrt{
    Rq_{22}
    +\dfrac{2\ell R^2}{r^2}
    \left(
    g+\sqrt{g^2+\dfrac{r^2q_{11}}{R}}
    \right)
    }\;
\end{bmatrix}
\]

\newpage

Now, it goes without saying, the corresponding control law given by $\dot\omega=-K[\theta,\dot\theta]^T$, where $K=R^{-1}B^TP$, is only so effective as the linearization error is small. With this in mind, we will now simulate the system \emph{close} to the equilibrium point. 
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Consider the inverted pendulum with mass $m=1\,\mathrm{kg}$, pendulum length $\ell=0.125\,\mathrm{m}$, and with wheel spoke radii $r=0.051\,\mathrm{m}$. In conjunction, let the state be initialized by $(\theta_0,\dot\theta_0)=(0.35\,\mathrm{rad},0.1\,\mathrm{rad/s})$. We also assume the uniform gravitational field to be $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$. Likewise, let the running cost be characterized by the parameters $q_{11}=1\,\mathrm{W}$, $q_{22}=1\,\mathrm{J\,s}$, and $R=10\,\mathrm{J\,s^3}$, which makes $J$ an energy cost functional that prioritizes minimizing the controller effort over equilibrium state error.
\vspace{3 mm}\\
With all said, the $A$ and $B$ matrices will be presented, and upon solving the ARE, the corresponding $P$ and $K$ matrices will be outlined as well:
\begin{align*}
A &=
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;0 & 1\;\\
    \;78.48\,\mathrm{s^{-2}} & 0\;
\end{bmatrix}
&
B &=
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;0\;\\
    \;-0.408\;
\end{bmatrix}
\\[6mm]
P &=
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;8.3533\times10^4\,\mathrm{J}
    &
    0.9429\times10^4\,\mathrm{J\,s}\;
    \\
   \;0.9429\times10^4\,\mathrm{J\,s}
    &
    0.1064\times10^4\,\mathrm{J\,s^2}\;
\end{bmatrix}
&
K &=
\begin{bmatrix}
    \;-384.7061\,\mathrm{W/s}
    &
    -43.4271\,\mathrm{W}\;
\end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}
The control algorithm derived from LQR can now be simulated. In particular, using the control systems toolbox in MATLAB (see Appendix), the state evolution can be illustrated in phase space accordingly: 
